# swarm-os — third-party deployment config.
#
# YOU DO NOT HAVE TO EDIT THIS BY HAND. `./install.sh` on a host with no `.env`
# runs a guided setup that asks for each value below, checks it against the
# host, and writes the file for you (`./install.sh --reconfigure` to redo it).
# This file is the reference for what it writes, and the manual path:
#     cp .env.example .env     # edit the values marked "EDIT"
#     ./install.sh             # or: set -a && source .env && set +a &&
#                              #     docker compose -f examples/docker-compose.dist.yml up -d
#
# This file is read by install.sh with `source`, so values may use ${HOME}.
# Keep one KEY=VALUE per line, no spaces around `=`, no inline comments.

# ── Image — SET IT EXPLICITLY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GHCR image to pull. Ask the maintainer for the exact tag to use.
# Pulling a private package requires a prior `docker login ghcr.io` with a
# GitHub PAT that has the `read:packages` scope (see docs/deploy/install.en.md).
#
# The compose file writes `image: ${SWARM_OS_IMAGE:-ghcr.io/nyyyxius/swarm-os:latest}`,
# so leaving this unset still starts a container — silently, off that fallback.
# Do not rely on it: this line is the one a one-click update REWRITES in place
# (a `.bak` is left beside this file), so an absent variable means the UI
# refuses to self-update (`IMAGE TAG IS HARDCODED`), and a single-segment value
# like `swarm-os:latest` is a locally-built image with nothing to pull
# (`IMAGE IS LOCALLY BUILT`). It is also how the container reports what it runs.
SWARM_OS_IMAGE=ghcr.io/nyyyxius/swarm-os:latest

# ── Update detection (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub PAT used to READ the releases of Nyyyxius/swarm-os. That repository
# is private, so an unauthenticated call answers 404: without this token the
# check stays on `no_token` and no new version is ever announced. Read-only, and
# never sent anywhere but api.github.com.
#   * classic PAT → scope `repo`; fine-grained PAT → `Contents: Read` on the
#     swarm-os repository.
#   * NOT the `read:packages` PAT used by `docker login` — that one cannot read
#     releases (one token may carry both scopes; swarm-os reads this variable).
# Empty is a healthy state: it means "never tell me about releases".
SWARM_OS_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── Identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shown in the UI, logs and Discord notifications. One instance == one project.
SWARM_OS_PROJECT_NAME=my-project
# Docker container name. Must be unique per instance on the host.
SWARM_OS_CONTAINER_NAME=swarm-os

# ── Run-as account ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHICH HOST ACCOUNT THIS INSTANCE BELONGS TO. The container process carries
# this uid:gid (docker-compose `user:`), so everything it writes under
# DATA_PATH and WORKSPACE_PATH belongs to that account — and under the ssh
# transport the agent CLIs are literally started as it.
#
# Unset means 1000:1000, the image's own `node` user: the historical behaviour,
# right whenever you install as the account that owns ~/.claude. Set them to
# hand the instance to a DIFFERENT account (`id -u alice`, `id -g alice`), and
# point CLAUDE_DIR / CLAUDE_JSON / WORKSPACE_PATH / DATA_PATH at that account's
# home. `setup.sh` asks the question and writes all of it for you.
# NEVER 0: the container must not run as root.
# SWARM_OS_RUN_UID=1001
# SWARM_OS_RUN_GID=1001
# Informational, for the installer only — it is how a reconfigure keeps this
# account instead of reverting to whoever re-runs it. Nothing reads it at
# runtime.
# SWARM_OS_RUN_USER=alice
# HOME *inside* the container. uid 1000 owns /home/node in the image; any other
# uid has no passwd entry there, gets HOME=/ and fails every npm / plugin
# install that writes under it. Point it into /data — the tree that account
# owns — and create the directory first (`mkdir -p ${DATA_PATH}/home`).
# SWARM_OS_CONTAINER_HOME=/data/home

# ── Host paths — EDIT THESE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All four belong to the run-as account above: it is the uid that reads and
# writes them.
# The project the agents work in (read-write).
WORKSPACE_PATH=${HOME}/dev/my-project
# Persistent state: SQLite DB, auth tokens, logs/, backups/, .claude-runtime/.
DATA_PATH=${HOME}/.swarm-os/my-project
# Your authenticated Claude Code dir, mounted READ-ONLY. REQUIRED.
# You must have run the Claude Code CLI and logged in at least once so that
# ${CLAUDE_DIR}/.credentials.json exists — agents cannot start otherwise.
CLAUDE_DIR=${HOME}/.claude
# Claude theme/auth file (read-only). Prevents the first-spawn theme picker
# from freezing a session. Leave as-is on a standard install.
CLAUDE_JSON=${HOME}/.claude.json

# ── Ports (host loopback only — NEVER bind 0.0.0.0) ───────────────────────────
# Host-side port for the web UI. The container always listens on 8787 inside.
SWARM_OS_PORT=8787
# In-container port for the VS Code web editor. NOT published on the host:
# swarm-os reverse-proxies the editor under /ide on SWARM_OS_PORT, same origin,
# so whatever ingress serves the UI serves the editor too. Change it only on a
# port clash inside the container. Turn the editor off from the Plugins screen;
# SWARM_OS_IDE_ENABLED (below) only seeds that choice at the first boot.
SWARM_OS_IDE_PORT=8790

# ── Agent transport ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHERE THE AGENT CLI PROCESSES RUN. This one variable decides three things at
# once, so it is worth a minute:
#
#   local (default) — agents are processes inside the container. The image only
#     carries the claude CLI, so claude is the only provider that can run;
#     SETTINGS ▸ SYSTEM UPDATE refuses (`TRANSPORT IS NOT SSH`) because nothing
#     in here can run `docker` on the host; plugin host-exec is unavailable.
#   ssh — the container opens `ssh -tt` and the agent runs ON THIS HOST with the
#     host's own logins. All four provider CLIs become usable, host and
#     container can never race on the same OAuth refresh, and one-click updates
#     work. It needs the compose overlay:
#         docker compose -f examples/docker-compose.dist.yml \
#                        -f examples/docker-compose.ssh.yml up -d
#     `./install.sh` adds that -f by itself when it reads ssh here.
SWARM_OS_AGENT_TRANSPORT=local

# The rest of this block is read ONLY under `ssh` (install.sh fills it in, key
# included — see docs/deploy/install.en.md §3).
# user@host as resolved FROM THE CONTAINER. `host.docker.internal` is mapped to
# the docker bridge gateway by the overlay's extra_hosts.
# SWARM_OS_SSH_TARGET=you@host.docker.internal
# SWARM_OS_SSH_PORT=22
# Directory mounted read-only at /host-ssh. Prefer a directory holding ONLY the
# swarm-os key over your whole ~/.ssh — the container can read all of it. The
# key file itself must be readable by uid 1000 (the image runs as `node`).
# HOST_SSH_DIR=${HOME}/.swarm-os/ssh
# Path to the key INSIDE the container, i.e. under /host-ssh.
# SWARM_OS_SSH_KEY=/host-ssh/swarm-os-spawner
# Host-side twins of the bind mounts: the agent resolves paths in the HOST's
# namespace, where /data and /workspace do not exist.
# SWARM_OS_HOST_DATA_DIR=${HOME}/.swarm-os/my-project
# SWARM_OS_HOST_WORKSPACE_ROOT=${HOME}/dev/my-project
# SWARM_OS_HOST_CLAUDE_DIR=${HOME}/.claude

# ── Remote access / notifications (optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# Base URL the operator reaches the UI at — used for Discord deep-links only
# (no token is ever included). e.g. https://my-host.tailnet.ts.net:8787
SWARM_OS_PUBLIC_URL=
# Discord webhook for idle / orchestrator-ended notifications. Empty = disabled.
SWARM_OS_DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ── Agent providers — multi-provider (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# Agents can run on four CLIs: `claude` (Anthropic), `codex` (OpenAI), `gemini`
# (Google) and `opencode` (models via OpenRouter). The provider is picked per
# team AND per agent, so mixed teams are possible. Full guide:
# docs/providers.md.
#
# Two host facts decide whether the non-claude ones are usable at all:
#   * the image ships ONLY the claude CLI, so under the default `local`
#     transport (what docker-compose.dist.yml does) the other three report
#     themselves unavailable — that is correct, not a misconfiguration;
#   * under the `ssh` transport the agent process runs on the HOST and reuses
#     the host's own credentials, so each CLI must be installed AND logged in
#     there (`codex login`, `gemini`, `claude`). swarm-os ships no API key.
# A CLI installed on the host becomes selectable at the next availability poll
# (60 s) — no container restart.

# Provider used when neither the request nor the role names one. Built-in ids
# only: claude|codex|gemini|opencode. Validated at boot — an unknown value
# stops the server instead of silently falling back.
# SWARM_OS_DEFAULT_PROVIDER=claude

# Host-side state directories the per-session credential symlinks point at, one
# per CLI (siblings of SWARM_OS_HOST_CLAUDE_DIR, ssh transport only).
# WARNING: their default is derived from the CONTAINER's home (/home/node/.codex
# and friends), which does not exist on the host. Left unset under ssh, the
# symlinks dangle, the agent starts with no visible error, and its first turn
# fails on an authentication error. Set them explicitly — and make sure the
# compose service names them in `environment:`, which is an allow-list: a
# variable not named there never reaches the process.
# SWARM_OS_HOST_CODEX_DIR=${HOME}/.codex
# SWARM_OS_HOST_GEMINI_DIR=${HOME}/.gemini
# SWARM_OS_HOST_OPENCODE_DIR=${HOME}/.opencode

# Which authentication method a gemini agent pins (gemini's own
# `security.auth.selectedType`): oauth-personal | gemini-api-key | vertex-ai |
# compute-default-credentials. gemini has no "use whatever works" mode — an
# unpinned value opens the auth-method picker, a modal that repaints for ever so
# the agent is never idle and never receives its team messages.
# SET IT UNDER THE ssh TRANSPORT: the container cannot see the host's ~/.gemini,
# so it cannot tell whether oauth_creds.json is there, and it defaults to
# `gemini-api-key` (a failure that at least shows up in the PTY). If the host is
# logged in with a Google account, say so here.
# SWARM_OS_GEMINI_AUTH_TYPE=oauth-personal

# OpenRouter API key used by `opencode` agents (its only credential mechanism —
# there is no `opencode login`). THE MAIN PATH IS THE UI: SETTINGS ▸ OPENROUTER
# stores it server-side, masks it on read, and applies it to the next agent
# spawn with no restart. This variable is only a dev/CI fallback for a
# deployment with no UI to type into: it is used when NO key is stored, and a
# stored key always wins over it. With neither, opencode reads as unavailable
# ("installed but no OpenRouter API key is configured").
# SWARM_OS_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# ── Optional overrides (defaults applied by docker-compose.dist.yml) ──────────
# SWARM_OS_DEFAULT_MODEL=claude-opus-5   # unset = the provider's own default, kept current
#                                        # from models.dev (see docs/providers.md)
# SWARM_OS_MODEL_CATALOGUE_URL=off       # freeze the model lists on the shipped pins,
#                                        # no outbound call. Default: https://models.dev/api.json
# SWARM_OS_MODEL_CATALOGUE_TTL_MS=21600000  # how often to refresh it (default 6 h)
# SWARM_OS_DEFAULT_EFFORT=xhigh          # low|medium|high|xhigh|max (xhigh is costly)
# SWARM_OS_MAX_ORCHESTRATORS=10
# SWARM_OS_MAX_WORKERS_PER_ORCH=20
# SWARM_OS_LOG_LEVEL=info                # trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal
# SWARM_OS_IDE_ENABLED=true              # FIRST-BOOT SEED ONLY for the VS Code web editor.
#                                        # After the first boot the database is the source
#                                        # of truth: enable/disable the IDE from the Plugins
#                                        # screen (BUILT-IN section — see docs/builtin-features.md).
#                                        # Editing this var later has no effect (the boot logs
#                                        # feature.seed_ignored when it disagrees with the DB).
